{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R57"],
      "pins": ["R57.1", "R57.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTN", "VOUTP"],
      "observed": "R57's schematic Value field is '24k' (and the SPICE deck uses 24k), but the same component's MPN is 'FRC0805J240TSD' (EIA 240 = 24 Ω) and its Description reads '24Ω ±5%' in an R_0805 (125 mW) footprint. R57 is wired directly across the rectified DC input rails VOUTP and VOUTN.",
      "expected": "The value field, MPN and description must agree. R57 sits as a bleed/discharge resistor across the bridge-rectifier DC output (VOUTP/VOUTN), so a high value (~24 kΩ) is intended; a 24 Ω part in 0805 would dissipate ~6 W at 12 V (far above the 125 mW rating) and heavily load the supply, which is not a sensible design.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R57\") (value \"24k\") ... (description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 ...\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") (value \"FRC0805J240TSD\"))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"222\") (name \"VOUTN\") ... (node (ref \"R57\") (pin \"1\") ...))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"223\") (name \"VOUTP\") ... (node (ref \"R57\") (pin \"2\") ...))"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "R57 VOUTN VOUTP 24k"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the board is assembled to the MPN/description (24 Ω) rather than the schematic value (24 kΩ), a 24 Ω load is placed across the rectified motor/DC input, drawing ~0.5 A at 12 V (~6 W) in a 125 mW 0805 package — overheating/burning the resistor and loading the supply, deviating from the intended high-value bleed.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Most of the design matches the spec (16 MHz MCP2515 crystals Y3/Y5, 14.7456 MHz UART crystals Y2/Y4, SM712 TVS on CAN, LTC4412+PMOS ideal-diode, SN74LVC1G07 e-stop buffers). The one clear deviation is R57, whose schematic value (24 k) contradicts its MPN/description (24 Ω) across the rectified DC input — a 1000× mismatch that, if sourced per the MPN, would put an over-dissipating load on the power input."
}

